WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.605
This is the technical deep dive for Book
09 of the Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:09.166
Network and Transport, and it's
for the network engineers and

4
00:00:09.166 --> 00:00:13.225
architects who'll actually move
branches off hairpinned transport.

5
00:00:13.225 --> 00:00:17.555
The whole book rests on one idea:
transport is a physics decision,

6
00:00:17.555 --> 00:00:20.087
and control is a compliance decision.

7
00:00:20.087 --> 00:00:22.552
Mix them up, or do them
in the wrong order,

8
00:00:22.552 --> 00:00:25.787
and a faster network becomes
a compliance finding.

9
00:00:25.787 --> 00:00:31.321
Part 1 covers policy and transport:
what TIC 3.0 really requires,

10
00:00:31.321 --> 00:00:35.800
then SD-WAN, SASE and the legacy
protocols riding on new paths.

11
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:41.084
Part 2 covers access and architecture:
802.1X after Active Directory,

12
00:00:41.084 --> 00:00:45.163
then the reference design
and who owns each decision.

13
00:00:45.163 --> 00:00:46.760
Let's start with why.

14
00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:48.897
Here's why this matters.

15
00:00:48.897 --> 00:00:52.559
Light in fiber covers about two
hundred kilometers per millisecond,

16
00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:54.486
and nothing you can buy changes that.

17
00:00:54.486 --> 00:00:57.965
The famous 150-millisecond
voice budget is mouth to ear,

18
00:00:57.965 --> 00:01:00.916
so the network really gets
seventy to a hundred,

19
00:01:00.916 --> 00:01:05.985
and a cross-country hairpin can burn most
of it before a call goes anywhere useful.

20
00:01:05.985 --> 00:01:07.235
That's the physics.

21
00:01:07.235 --> 00:01:09.648
The compliance side is just as firm:

22
00:01:09.648 --> 00:01:14.972
a circuit closes exactly one control
family, telecommunications diversity.

23
00:01:14.972 --> 00:01:18.172
Everything else comes from the
layer that governs the circuit.

24
00:01:18.172 --> 00:01:22.510
So a bare local breakout fixes
latency and loses the boundary.

25
00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:25.510
Which brings us to the
order you do things in.

26
00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:30.048
This is the spine of the whole
deck: an order of operations.

27
00:01:30.048 --> 00:01:34.791
Step zero is securing the governor
itself, because an SD-WAN controller

28
00:01:34.791 --> 00:01:38.972
is an internet-facing system with
authority over every branch.

29
00:01:38.972 --> 00:01:43.564
Then buy the pipe on measured physics,
get the governor live and logging,

30
00:01:43.564 --> 00:01:46.366
decide the boundary change before cutover,

31
00:01:46.366 --> 00:01:51.191
handle Microsoft 365 through its own
exception, and register the evidence,

32
00:01:51.191 --> 00:01:55.160
including CISA's telemetry,
before any old circuit goes away.

33
00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.894
The book is candid that this
is an engineering dependency,

34
00:01:58.894 --> 00:02:02.472
not a mandate, and that's exactly
why it holds up in review.

35
00:02:02.472 --> 00:02:04.060
The table is our map.

36
00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:07.480
Module 1 starts with what
policy actually says.

37
00:02:08.230 --> 00:02:11.143
Module 1 is TIC 3.0 in practice.

38
00:02:11.143 --> 00:02:13.941
Branch decisions get
argued in policy terms,

39
00:02:13.941 --> 00:02:18.480
and most of those arguments don't
survive a careful read of the documents.

40
00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.935
But the opposite mistake
is just as common:

41
00:02:20.935 --> 00:02:24.092
assuming technology-neutral
means requirement-free.

42
00:02:24.092 --> 00:02:25.675
We'll read what's really there,

43
00:02:25.675 --> 00:02:29.460
and finish with how token security
changes where data can live.

44
00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:33.517
Let's read TIC 3.0 from
the documents themselves,

45
00:02:33.517 --> 00:02:35.435
not from what people say about them.

46
00:02:35.435 --> 00:02:38.539
There are four use cases,
and no SASE use case;

47
00:02:38.539 --> 00:02:42.985
CISA's SASE guide describes an
approach, it doesn't require one.

48
00:02:42.985 --> 00:02:45.360
What constrains a branch are two rules.

49
00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:50.973
First, policy enforcement parity: if you
need a capability anywhere, you need it on

50
00:02:50.973 --> 00:02:56.335
every path, and that's the only place TIC
mentions SD-WAN, as something to govern.

51
00:02:56.335 --> 00:03:00.710
Second, CISA keeps its visibility
when branches break out locally.

52
00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:04.985
So technology-neutral gives you
discretion, with an obligation attached.

53
00:03:04.985 --> 00:03:08.400
And binding instruments
sit right beside TIC.

54
00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:13.050
This slide is the answer to
"TIC doesn't require anything."

55
00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:16.775
Four binding obligations land
directly on branch equipment.

56
00:03:16.775 --> 00:03:20.184
Egress DNS has to reach
CISA's Protective DNS.

57
00:03:20.184 --> 00:03:24.400
Management interfaces can't
sit exposed on the internet.

58
00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.975
End-of-support edge devices
come out on a dated schedule.

59
00:03:27.975 --> 00:03:33.950
And anything terminating TLS or IPsec
has to support post-quantum key exchange.

60
00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:37.475
None of them picks a product,
but together they shape the box.

61
00:03:37.475 --> 00:03:41.789
The big takeaway is the refresh:
replacing branch hardware is already a

62
00:03:41.789 --> 00:03:46.250
dated obligation, so that purchase is
where the architecture gets chosen.

63
00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:50.560
Next, the controls an agency
asserts about its own boundary.

64
00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:55.410
The hardest test at a branch is
often one the agency wrote itself.

65
00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:58.955
Once a branch breaks out locally,
it's an external boundary,

66
00:03:58.955 --> 00:04:03.090
and the security plan makes promises
about it: boundary monitoring,

67
00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:06.635
a traffic-flow policy per
service, continuous monitoring.

68
00:04:06.635 --> 00:04:11.747
Lacking SD-WAN isn't a finding; being
unable to produce that evidence is.

69
00:04:11.747 --> 00:04:14.386
On encryption, the book is precise.

70
00:04:14.386 --> 00:04:19.110
The controls accept TLS or IPsec,
and nothing in the baselines demands

71
00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:23.510
an encrypted WAN when the payload
is already protected end to end.

72
00:04:23.510 --> 00:04:27.007
Where encryption really
matters is cleartext protocols,

73
00:04:27.007 --> 00:04:30.723
so inventory those and treat the
list as an exposure register.

74
00:04:30.723 --> 00:04:33.990
Now, what happens to DNS and telemetry.

75
00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:39.065
When traffic stops passing the old
access point, three things move with it.

76
00:04:39.065 --> 00:04:43.478
DNS still has to end at CISA, and
the trap is quietly forwarding to a

77
00:04:43.478 --> 00:04:48.452
vendor's look-alike resolver: everything
works, and you're out of compliance.

78
00:04:48.452 --> 00:04:55.140
Logging now runs under M-26-14, with
maturity dates already on the calendar.

79
00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:59.362
And CISA's telemetry, which used to
come from sensors on the MTIPS path,

80
00:04:59.362 --> 00:05:01.328
has to come through CLAW instead.

81
00:05:01.328 --> 00:05:03.694
The rule the book recommends is simple:

82
00:05:03.694 --> 00:05:08.577
don't turn off MTIPS until CISA confirms
the replacement telemetry is flowing.

83
00:05:08.577 --> 00:05:11.450
Then a quick check on which
policies are still current.

84
00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:16.168
A lot of network documents carry
at least one stale citation,

85
00:05:16.168 --> 00:05:19.025
so here's where things
stand in October 2026.

86
00:05:19.025 --> 00:05:24.093
M-21-31 is gone, older vulnerability
directives have been replaced,

87
00:05:24.093 --> 00:05:27.687
and the legacy telecom
contracts have expired.

88
00:05:27.687 --> 00:05:30.087
The FedRAMP point matters most day to day.

89
00:05:30.087 --> 00:05:34.176
FedRAMP certifies a cloud service,
not the route you take to reach it.

90
00:05:34.176 --> 00:05:37.950
So when someone refuses a network
change for FedRAMP reasons,

91
00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:39.800
ask which control it violates.

92
00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:42.453
There isn't one, and the
book treats a refusal

93
00:05:42.453 --> 00:05:45.400
without a control basis
as a governance problem.

94
00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:49.220
Now the data-side idea that
makes centralization safe.

95
00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:53.508
This slide explains why network
people should care about tokens.

96
00:05:53.508 --> 00:05:58.207
The old reason to split data into many
stores was isolation by perimeter.

97
00:05:58.207 --> 00:06:03.747
Tokens remove it: when every request
carries a verifiable identity, one store can

98
00:06:03.747 --> 00:06:09.369
safely serve many trust domains, and the
long-lived database password disappears.

99
00:06:09.369 --> 00:06:11.158
The physics still applies, though.

100
00:06:11.158 --> 00:06:14.769
Strong consistency over long
distances is expensive,

101
00:06:14.769 --> 00:06:19.183
so keep it inside a region and let
everything else be asynchronous.

102
00:06:19.183 --> 00:06:22.507
That choice decides which
flows need a fast path at all.

103
00:06:22.507 --> 00:06:26.822
And the price is named honestly:
isolation now lives in code,

104
00:06:26.822 --> 00:06:30.432
so that code has to be small,
tested and fail-closed.

105
00:06:30.432 --> 00:06:32.460
That wraps up Module 1.

106
00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:37.923
Module 2 is governed transport and
the legacy protocols that ride on it.

107
00:06:37.923 --> 00:06:43.285
First the governor itself: what SD-WAN and
SASE each close, and how to protect them.

108
00:06:43.285 --> 00:06:46.239
Then what actually breaks
when you re-route a branch,

109
00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:51.000
which turns out to be a shorter, and
different, list than the folklore suggests.

110
00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:54.525
Here's the honest version
of what SD-WAN does.

111
00:06:54.525 --> 00:06:59.469
It can't beat the speed of light, but most
real paths run well above that floor,

112
00:06:59.469 --> 00:07:02.100
and the gap is where
SD-WAN earns its keep.

113
00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:06.305
It separates lean, latency-sensitive
traffic from bulk transfers,

114
00:07:06.305 --> 00:07:09.363
and it recovers some of
the detour in real routes.

115
00:07:09.363 --> 00:07:11.363
The underrated part is the tail.

116
00:07:11.363 --> 00:07:15.799
A quorum commit or a voice call is
only as good as its unluckiest packet,

117
00:07:15.799 --> 00:07:21.000
and tools like forward error correction
and packet duplication shrink that tail.

118
00:07:21.000 --> 00:07:23.300
What it never does is go below the floor.

119
00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:25.550
So what does SASE add on top?

120
00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:29.875
SD-WAN decides paths; it
doesn't inspect anything.

121
00:07:29.875 --> 00:07:35.149
Boundary protection, web policy, malware
inspection and private-app access come from

122
00:07:35.149 --> 00:07:40.200
the SASE services above it, and this table
shows which layer closes which control.

123
00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:41.712
Two practical points.

124
00:07:41.712 --> 00:07:46.391
The enforcement point has a location,
so a distant or poorly peered point

125
00:07:46.391 --> 00:07:50.913
of presence is just a hairpin with a
new name; measure it before cutover.

126
00:07:50.913 --> 00:07:54.191
And FedRAMP status is per
offering, not per vendor,

127
00:07:54.191 --> 00:07:58.188
and it covers the cloud service,
not the boxes in your offices.

128
00:07:58.188 --> 00:08:02.380
Which raises the question nobody
likes: who protects the governor?

129
00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:05.835
The governor deserves
its own security program,

130
00:08:05.835 --> 00:08:09.555
because it becomes the most
consequential network system you own.

131
00:08:09.555 --> 00:08:13.004
SD-WAN trades a few hardened
egress points for hundreds of

132
00:08:13.004 --> 00:08:17.243
internet-facing edges and one
controller that can reroute everything.

133
00:08:17.243 --> 00:08:20.066
This isn't hypothetical: in February 2026,

134
00:08:20.066 --> 00:08:25.133
CISA issued an emergency directive
for one vendor's SD-WAN control plane,

135
00:08:25.133 --> 00:08:28.705
and its sequence makes a
good drill whatever you run.

136
00:08:28.705 --> 00:08:34.505
Inventory, preserve the logs, patch,
hunt, rebuild if needed, harden.

137
00:08:34.505 --> 00:08:36.943
The table is the minimum control set.

138
00:08:36.943 --> 00:08:40.817
Be able to run that drill before
production traffic depends on it.

139
00:08:40.817 --> 00:08:44.640
Next, where the program would
like to take the path itself.

140
00:08:45.390 --> 00:08:48.315
This slide is a proposal,
and it's labeled that way.

141
00:08:48.315 --> 00:08:52.398
The idea: today identity is
checked on every call to the data,

142
00:08:52.398 --> 00:08:56.065
but a network session is
trusted once and then coasts.

143
00:08:56.065 --> 00:09:01.103
The proposed governed path treats the
path itself like a token, leased for one

144
00:09:01.103 --> 00:09:06.140
purpose and expiring with it, so a flow
with no token behind it shows up as drift.

145
00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:10.760
Whether or not that's adopted, one
part applies now: SD-WAN and SASE

146
00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:15.978
decisions should land as records, with
identity, policy and time attached.

147
00:09:15.978 --> 00:09:17.402
That's your evidence later.

148
00:09:17.402 --> 00:09:20.030
Now, what breaks when paths change.

149
00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:25.793
Re-routing a branch does break things,
but for specific, documented reasons.

150
00:09:25.793 --> 00:09:30.523
Proxies time out idle connections,
inspection breaks pinned certificates

151
00:09:30.523 --> 00:09:34.954
and mutual TLS, and some applications
care about the client's IP address.

152
00:09:34.954 --> 00:09:36.917
That's the list to design around.

153
00:09:36.917 --> 00:09:39.143
Just as useful is what doesn't break.

154
00:09:39.143 --> 00:09:43.660
A path change doesn't scramble TCP;
a changed source address does,

155
00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:47.155
so address continuity is
the property to protect.

156
00:09:47.155 --> 00:09:51.935
And Kerberos doesn't check client
addresses by default, doesn't need a domain

157
00:09:51.935 --> 00:09:56.942
controller within milliseconds, and no
policy mandates short token lifetimes.

158
00:09:56.942 --> 00:09:59.542
Wrong mechanisms lead to wrong fixes.

159
00:09:59.542 --> 00:10:02.840
The real Kerberos dependency is next.

160
00:10:03.590 --> 00:10:06.882
This is the slide behind the
classic help-desk report:

161
00:10:06.882 --> 00:10:09.127
everything works except signing in.

162
00:10:09.127 --> 00:10:13.379
A handful of operations still need
a reachable domain controller,

163
00:10:13.379 --> 00:10:16.703
like first logon, Group
Policy and password changes.

164
00:10:16.703 --> 00:10:20.275
A branch on local breakout
reaches the internet just fine,

165
00:10:20.275 --> 00:10:24.964
but if nobody planned its path to a
domain controller, those operations fail.

166
00:10:24.964 --> 00:10:27.918
Worse, Windows quietly falls back to NTLM,

167
00:10:27.918 --> 00:10:31.740
which is exactly what the
program is trying to retire.

168
00:10:31.740 --> 00:10:35.859
So give every branch a defined
path, through a private link,

169
00:10:35.859 --> 00:10:40.578
a connector or a KDC proxy, or remove
the dependency with Entra join.

170
00:10:40.578 --> 00:10:43.560
Then, a reality check on performance.

171
00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:45.973
Two practical lessons here.

172
00:10:45.973 --> 00:10:50.610
First, for chatty applications,
transport improvements help modestly,

173
00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:55.484
about a fifth, because a branch device
only touches the first leg of the trip.

174
00:10:55.484 --> 00:10:59.135
Cutting thirty round trips down
to three helps about tenfold.

175
00:10:59.135 --> 00:11:04.110
So the application change usually wins,
and that belongs to the application owner.

176
00:11:04.110 --> 00:11:06.213
Second, for token-based access,

177
00:11:06.213 --> 00:11:10.335
the network matters through a
stable, known egress address.

178
00:11:10.335 --> 00:11:14.304
Conditional Access and continuous
access evaluation rely on

179
00:11:14.304 --> 00:11:18.273
seeing the same addresses for
sign-in and for resource traffic.

180
00:11:18.273 --> 00:11:22.911
Unpredictable egress means false
alarms and weaker location policy.

181
00:11:22.911 --> 00:11:24.660
That closes Part 1.

182
00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:29.735
Module 3 is network access
control after Active Directory.

183
00:11:29.735 --> 00:11:33.035
Sign-in controls can't stop
someone who simply plugs a

184
00:11:33.035 --> 00:11:36.260
laptop into a conference-room
jack; only the port can.

185
00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:39.461
And the RADIUS server checking
that port is usually the

186
00:11:39.461 --> 00:11:43.010
last piece of the network still
standing on Active Directory.

187
00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:48.120
So this module is about proving devices,
and retiring that dependency safely.

188
00:11:48.870 --> 00:11:52.411
Port admission has three
players: software on the device,

189
00:11:52.411 --> 00:11:57.007
the switch or access point that relays,
and the RADIUS server that decides.

190
00:11:57.007 --> 00:12:01.039
The method that matters is EAP-TLS,
a certificate on each side,

191
00:12:01.039 --> 00:12:05.795
so neither the device nor the network
can be faked without a private key.

192
00:12:05.795 --> 00:12:08.270
The urgency comes from Windows itself.

193
00:12:08.270 --> 00:12:14.052
Password-based methods like PEAP with
MS-CHAPv2 are being cut off from single

194
00:12:14.052 --> 00:12:19.383
sign-on, first by Credential Guard
and now by the October 2026 hardening.

195
00:12:19.383 --> 00:12:24.044
So agencies still on password-based
Wi-Fi have a deadline they didn't choose.

196
00:12:24.044 --> 00:12:26.700
Next, the server doing the checking.

197
00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:31.525
Windows NPS works today because
devices are hybrid-joined.

198
00:12:31.525 --> 00:12:35.134
The trouble is that it leans on
Active Directory computer objects,

199
00:12:35.134 --> 00:12:37.300
and Entra-joined devices don't have them.

200
00:12:37.300 --> 00:12:40.589
Entra ID offers no RADIUS
service of its own,

201
00:12:40.589 --> 00:12:43.513
and NPS has no way to read device health.

202
00:12:43.513 --> 00:12:47.176
So NPS gets less useful
with every device you move.

203
00:12:47.176 --> 00:12:52.232
The options are an on-premises NAC platform,
a cloud RADIUS service with the right

204
00:12:52.232 --> 00:12:57.288
FedRAMP status for that function, or taking
RADIUS out of remote access entirely.

205
00:12:57.288 --> 00:13:01.056
The program's rule: tie the
switch to the migration timeline,

206
00:13:01.056 --> 00:13:03.287
and authorize cloud devices first.

207
00:13:03.287 --> 00:13:06.050
Next, where the health signal comes from.

208
00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:11.050
The program doesn't add a separate
posture probe; it reads Intune.

209
00:13:11.050 --> 00:13:16.983
A NAC product registers with Entra ID,
the device certificate carries its Intune

210
00:13:16.983 --> 00:13:22.562
device ID, and on connect the NAC asks
Intune whether the device is compliant.

211
00:13:22.562 --> 00:13:24.649
Two realities to plan for.

212
00:13:24.649 --> 00:13:29.613
Posture is only as fresh as Intune's
last check-in, which can be hours old.

213
00:13:29.613 --> 00:13:32.415
And when a device falls out
of compliance mid-shift,

214
00:13:32.415 --> 00:13:35.951
something has to move the port,
which is Change of Authorization.

215
00:13:35.951 --> 00:13:39.868
NPS can't send it, and building
your own is real engineering,

216
00:13:39.868 --> 00:13:42.888
which is a strong argument
for a NAC platform.

217
00:13:42.888 --> 00:13:44.990
So where do devices land?

218
00:13:45.740 --> 00:13:50.140
Every device lands in one of five
segments, and this flow shows how.

219
00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:52.615
No valid certificate means quarantine.

220
00:13:52.615 --> 00:13:57.379
A good certificate but failed compliance
means remediation, where the device

221
00:13:57.379 --> 00:14:01.703
can fix itself and come back, and
never drifts silently into production.

222
00:14:01.703 --> 00:14:05.902
Devices that can't run a supplicant,
like badge readers and printers,

223
00:14:05.902 --> 00:14:07.777
are classified by MAC address,

224
00:14:07.777 --> 00:14:12.127
and the book is blunt that this is
classification, not authentication.

225
00:14:12.127 --> 00:14:16.365
The eight-hour re-authentication is
the backstop when nothing else fires.

226
00:14:16.365 --> 00:14:19.090
Next, hardening RADIUS itself.

227
00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:22.052
RADIUS has a weakness of its own.

228
00:14:22.052 --> 00:14:26.002
Over plain UDP it relies on
MD5, and the 2024 Blast-RADIUS

229
00:14:26.002 --> 00:14:30.553
research showed an attacker in
the middle could forge an accept.

230
00:14:30.553 --> 00:14:33.619
EAP is largely protected,
but the fix is cheap:

231
00:14:33.619 --> 00:14:38.842
require the message authenticator
everywhere, use strong rotated secrets,

232
00:14:38.842 --> 00:14:42.490
and move to RADIUS over TLS
where the hardware allows.

233
00:14:42.490 --> 00:14:46.951
On the wire, one certificate
serves wired and wireless,

234
00:14:46.951 --> 00:14:49.727
and WPA3-Enterprise is the target.

235
00:14:49.727 --> 00:14:55.853
And don't forget the server certificates:
they renew through the lifecycle platform,

236
00:14:55.853 --> 00:14:59.091
and one, the NPS MFA
extension's, is easy to miss.

237
00:14:59.091 --> 00:15:01.990
Now, rolling this out without an outage.

238
00:15:02.740 --> 00:15:05.756
The fastest way to cause an
outage with access control

239
00:15:05.756 --> 00:15:08.503
is to enforce before you
know what's on the network.

240
00:15:08.503 --> 00:15:12.545
So the rollout starts in monitor
mode: everything authenticates,

241
00:15:12.545 --> 00:15:15.539
nothing is blocked, and
the logs build a baseline.

242
00:15:15.539 --> 00:15:20.222
Then wired enforcement, then wireless,
then tightening the device segments,

243
00:15:20.222 --> 00:15:25.202
finishing with a tabletop where someone
plugs a rogue laptop into a meeting room.

244
00:15:25.202 --> 00:15:30.052
Done this way, it closes real
device-authentication and inventory controls.

245
00:15:30.052 --> 00:15:32.959
One correction worth knowing:
it doesn't close the

246
00:15:32.959 --> 00:15:36.290
external-connections control
people often claim for it.

247
00:15:36.290 --> 00:15:38.630
That wraps up access control.

248
00:15:39.380 --> 00:15:43.603
Module 4 pulls everything together
into a reference architecture,

249
00:15:43.603 --> 00:15:48.430
then answers the question behind most
of the expensive mistakes: who decides.

250
00:15:48.430 --> 00:15:51.631
Circuits get bought before
anyone owns the governor,

251
00:15:51.631 --> 00:15:55.342
and cutovers happen before anyone
files the boundary change.

252
00:15:55.342 --> 00:15:59.290
Naming one decider per step
costs less than any product.

253
00:16:00.040 --> 00:16:03.278
One rule ties the data and
network layers together:

254
00:16:03.278 --> 00:16:07.440
match each flow's consistency needs
to the transport built for it.

255
00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:10.663
Strongly consistent
writes and their quorum

256
00:16:10.663 --> 00:16:13.378
stay inside one region, on the fast path.

257
00:16:13.378 --> 00:16:18.202
Everything that crosses regions is
asynchronous and rides the bulk path.

258
00:16:18.202 --> 00:16:21.577
That's not a limitation; it's
using physics on purpose.

259
00:16:21.577 --> 00:16:26.481
The program's own SaaS data plane is
the worked example: one regional writer

260
00:16:26.481 --> 00:16:31.702
and tokens on both sides, so SD-WAN would
matter only for background replication.

261
00:16:31.702 --> 00:16:33.870
Now the same rule at a branch.

262
00:16:34.620 --> 00:16:37.510
At a branch, the question
is where traffic leaves

263
00:16:37.510 --> 00:16:40.545
the building and what
enforces policy when it does.

264
00:16:40.545 --> 00:16:41.970
There are four patterns.

265
00:16:41.970 --> 00:16:44.145
The hairpin is assessed but slow.

266
00:16:44.145 --> 00:16:46.795
Bare breakout is fast, and a regression.

267
00:16:46.795 --> 00:16:49.339
Governed breakout, with
an edge device and a

268
00:16:49.339 --> 00:16:52.308
nearby security service,
is the reference pattern.

269
00:16:52.308 --> 00:16:55.651
And agent-based SASE suits
small or roaming sites,

270
00:16:55.651 --> 00:16:59.233
as long as the nearest point
of presence really is near.

271
00:16:59.233 --> 00:17:03.581
The guidance is to choose per site
from measured latency and device mix,

272
00:17:03.581 --> 00:17:05.645
and never to choose bare breakout.

273
00:17:05.645 --> 00:17:08.370
Five decisions carry most of the risk.

274
00:17:09.120 --> 00:17:11.356
Most of the risk sits in five decisions,

275
00:17:11.356 --> 00:17:15.758
and what makes them dangerous is that
skipping one breaks nothing on the day.

276
00:17:15.758 --> 00:17:17.446
Keep one writer per region.

277
00:17:17.446 --> 00:17:20.499
Write the path policy for
every application class,

278
00:17:20.499 --> 00:17:24.208
which is also the traffic-flow
policy an assessor will ask for.

279
00:17:24.208 --> 00:17:28.494
Pick the egress pattern per site,
and file the boundary change first.

280
00:17:28.494 --> 00:17:31.807
Bring the governor under control
before it carries traffic.

281
00:17:31.807 --> 00:17:33.920
And make the path itself evidence.

282
00:17:33.920 --> 00:17:37.433
Each one comes with a concrete
check, so you can prove it was done.

283
00:17:37.433 --> 00:17:39.320
Next, who owns them.

284
00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:43.132
Here's the part of the book
that's new: ownership.

285
00:17:43.132 --> 00:17:47.622
Many network decisions are really risk
decisions dressed up as engineering

286
00:17:47.622 --> 00:17:51.620
or purchasing, so they have to reach
the person who can accept the risk.

287
00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:54.058
This matrix gives one decider per row.

288
00:17:54.058 --> 00:17:55.507
A few are worth noticing.

289
00:17:55.507 --> 00:17:59.514
The authorizing official decides
that the boundary may change,

290
00:17:59.514 --> 00:18:03.746
but the system owner decides, site
by site, whether that site is ready.

291
00:18:03.746 --> 00:18:08.652
And a circuit ends only after security
operations confirms CISA's telemetry,

292
00:18:08.652 --> 00:18:11.370
and only the contracting
officer can end it.

293
00:18:11.370 --> 00:18:14.210
Next, a named gate for each step.

294
00:18:14.960 --> 00:18:18.565
Each step in the order of
operations now has one decider,

295
00:18:18.565 --> 00:18:21.622
and the gate between steps
is a person, not a date.

296
00:18:21.622 --> 00:18:24.513
The CISO signs off on
the governor's security,

297
00:18:24.513 --> 00:18:27.099
the system owner on each site's readiness,

298
00:18:27.099 --> 00:18:32.498
and the authorizing official on the boundary
change and the Microsoft 365 exception.

299
00:18:32.498 --> 00:18:37.476
The boundary change rides on evidence:
the path policy, failover tests, path

300
00:18:37.476 --> 00:18:40.286
telemetry, exceptions, CLAW confirmation,

301
00:18:40.286 --> 00:18:43.097
and an honest list of
what you can't show yet.

302
00:18:43.097 --> 00:18:46.290
Let's finish with what to
decide, and what to start.

303
00:18:47.040 --> 00:18:50.887
To close: five decisions to put in
front of the people who own them,

304
00:18:50.887 --> 00:18:54.008
from the governor platform
and the NPS exit trigger

305
00:18:54.008 --> 00:18:57.202
to the boundary change and
the rule for leaving MTIPS.

306
00:18:57.202 --> 00:19:00.603
And there's plenty you can start
on Monday without a new contract.

307
00:19:00.603 --> 00:19:03.728
Verify where your DNS really goes.

308
00:19:03.728 --> 00:19:06.303
Inventory cleartext protocols.

309
00:19:06.303 --> 00:19:09.377
Check each branch's path
to a domain controller.

310
00:19:09.377 --> 00:19:10.827
Write the path policy.

311
00:19:10.827 --> 00:19:13.752
Put the right attributes
into every refresh.

312
00:19:13.752 --> 00:19:17.977
Start CLAW onboarding
and 802.1X monitor mode.

313
00:19:17.977 --> 00:19:20.266
And measure a pair of
branches before you buy.

314
00:19:20.266 --> 00:19:25.728
Governor before traffic, boundary before
cutover, evidence before a circuit ends.

315
00:19:25.728 --> 00:19:26.540
Thank you.
